  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Eid al-Fitr, or the \"Festival of Breaking the Fast,\" is a major three-day Islamic holiday marking the end of Ramadan’s month-long dawn-to-sunset fasting. Celebrated on the first day of Shawwal (10th Islamic month), it signifies gratitude, charity (Zakat al-Fitr), community prayers, special meals, and gift-giving, honoring the completion of a period of spiritual reflection and patience. Key Details About Eid al-Fitr: Significance: It serves as a spiritual reward for completing Ramadan, allowing Muslims to celebrate with family and community. Origin: The holiday was established by the Prophet Muhammad, often focusing on gratitude for the strength to complete the fast, acting as a \"festival of thanksgiving\". Celebration Period: The festival officially lasts for three days, although the start date depends on the sighting of the new crescent moon. Traditions: Common practices include wearing new or clean clothes, performing special morning prayers, greeting one another with \"Eid Mubarak\" (Blessed Eid), giving to charity, and eating sweets. Date Variability: Because it follows the lunar Hijri calendar, the date of Eid al-Fitr changes every year, moving approximately 11 days earlier each time. It is important to note that fasting is strictly forbidden on the day of Eid al-Fitr.",
